From fd804a597e7ad38a19b554c89a4bdb5245b8368e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 16:50:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Let each application say what its own build answers to
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Nothing in this suite can see a build setting, and that is the gap the
last two days fell into. Every check reads the repository -- that the
committed plists agree with `HandOff`, that each application opens the
other's scheme and not its own -- and an `INFOPLIST_FILE` nobody
applied passes all of them while producing a binary that registers
nothing at all.

Its symptom is identical to a scheme spelled wrong, to a wallet that is
not installed, and to a hand-off never attempted: nothing happens, on
two screens, with no error on either. The whole repository was correct
and the phone did nothing, which is the worst shape a fault can have.

So both applications read their own `CFBundleURLTypes` at launch and
print it -- *This build · answers to · ovpoc-wallet* -- quietly when it
is right and in red with the one build setting named when it is not.
The wallet's is the one that matters, since the voting application
cannot see whether its sibling registered anything; the voting
application's own line is worth having because the two targets are
configured in the same sitting, so an application that does not answer
to its own scheme is one whose sibling almost certainly does not
either.

This is `service/build.py`'s argument one layer down. A thing that
cannot say what it is gets checked by proxies invented on the spot, and
those proxies are wrong in ways nobody notices.

`HandOff.schemes(in:)` is split out from `schemesRegistered(by:)`
because a `Bundle` with an arbitrary info dictionary is not something a
test can build, and the part that goes wrong is the reading of the
shape: absent, not an array, an entry naming itself and claiming no
scheme. `testTheRegisteredSchemesAreReadOutOfTheUrlTypes` requires each
of those to read as *nothing* rather than as a crash or a plausible
wrong answer -- a screen that exists to break a silence must not be
able to invent one.

The mutation is honest about what it establishes: removing the read
makes a broken build look like a working one, and it is caught by a
Python grep rather than by anything that compiles Swift.

